# Line-ending policy.
#
# Windows Git defaults to core.autocrlf=true, which rewrites LF to CRLF on
# checkout. For the shell scripts that ship inside the Linux container this is
# not cosmetic -- a CRLF shebang (#!/bin/sh\r) makes the kernel look for an
# interpreter literally named "/bin/sh\r", and the script fails to execute.
#
# docker/entrypoint.sh is COPYed into the image and executed by supervisor, so
# it must stay LF no matter which machine checks the repo out.
#
# Detection:  git ls-files --eol -- docker/entrypoint.sh scripts/

# Shell scripts and anything executed inside the container: always LF.
*.sh            text eol=lf
docker/*.conf   text eol=lf
Dockerfile      text eol=lf
.dockerignore   text eol=lf
*.yml           text eol=lf
*.yaml          text eol=lf

# PHP, JSON and Markdown: normalise to LF in the repository. PHP does not care,
# but a single convention avoids whole-file diffs when two machines disagree.
*.php           text eol=lf
*.json          text eol=lf
*.md            text eol=lf
*.xml           text eol=lf
*.ini           text eol=lf
*.css           text eol=lf
*.js            text eol=lf
*.blade.php     text eol=lf

# Windows-only tooling: keep the native CRLF so PowerShell and cmd are happy.
*.ps1           text eol=crlf
*.bat           text eol=crlf
*.cmd           text eol=crlf

# Binary: never touch these.
*.png           binary
*.jpg           binary
*.jpeg          binary
*.gif           binary
*.ico           binary
*.svg           text eol=lf
*.woff          binary
*.woff2         binary
